Subject: [PATCH] audio: use /dev/audio on substrate (Sun/SADA device path)

Substrate's audio device is the Sun/NetBSD-style /dev/audio (an alias for
/dev/audio0), not the OSS /dev/dsp.  substrate's <sys/audioio.h> matches
NetBSD 10's audio_info, so the netbsd backend is the one built for it (see
the CMake patch in this series), but SDL_audiodev.c only maps
SDL_PATH_DEV_DSP to "/dev/audio" for SDL_PLATFORM_NETBSD / _OPENBSD.

Without this, SDL_EnumUnixAudioDevices() probes /dev/dsp, which does not
exist, finds zero output devices, and opening the default device fails --
so no audio thread or callback ever runs.  (That is exactly what happened
to the SDL2 port before the equivalent fix.)

Add SDL_PLATFORM_SUBSTRATE to the condition.

--- a/src/audio/SDL_audiodev.c
+++ b/src/audio/SDL_audiodev.c
@@ -32,7 +32,7 @@
 #include "SDL_audiodev_c.h"
 
 #ifndef SDL_PATH_DEV_DSP
-#if defined(SDL_PLATFORM_NETBSD) || defined(SDL_PLATFORM_OPENBSD)
+#if defined(SDL_PLATFORM_NETBSD) || defined(SDL_PLATFORM_OPENBSD) || defined(SDL_PLATFORM_SUBSTRATE)
 #define SDL_PATH_DEV_DSP "/dev/audio"
 #else
 #define SDL_PATH_DEV_DSP "/dev/dsp"
